<p>This page defines technical terms and regulatory concepts used throughout this report. Most terms are relevant to understanding property impacts and the State Environmental Study process.</p>

<div class="glossary-grid">

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Activity Category B</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A Federal Highway Administration noise classification for residential land uses. The noise impact threshold for Activity Category B is 66 decibels (dBA), which triggers evaluation for sound wall mitigation.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Build Alternative</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      In the State Environmental Study process, the "Build Alternative" is the proposed highway configuration (alignment, footprint, interchange design) being evaluated. Alternatives Development determines the specific alignment and footprint.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Corridor Preservation</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A program by which UDOT acquires land in advance of a funded construction project to secure the right-of-way. Corridor Preservation is voluntary (property owners can decline UDOT's offer) on unfunded projects under Utah Code 72-5-407.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>dBA (Decibels)</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A unit of sound pressure level. In highway noise assessment, 66 dBA is the impact threshold for residential areas. For reference: normal conversation ~60 dBA; busy traffic ~70 dBA; lawnmower ~85 dBA.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Eminent Domain</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      The government's legal authority to acquire private property for public use with compensation. UDOT gains eminent domain authority for SR-177 Weber County extension only after the project enters the funded construction phase.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Feasibility Criterion (Noise)</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      For sound wall mitigation, feasibility requires that the wall achieve at least 5 decibels (dBA) of noise reduction for affected receivers. A wall failing the feasibility criterion will not be constructed.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Frontage Road</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A local road that runs parallel to a limited-access highway (like SR-177), providing access to adjacent properties that would otherwise be isolated by the highway. If UDOT selects an alignment along or near 5100 West, portions of existing local roads may need to serve frontage-road or access-road functions, but that configuration has not been finalized.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Grade-Separated Interchange</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A highway intersection where one roadway passes over or under another (via bridge or underpass) with ramps connecting the levels. Examples: overpasses, underpasses with merge ramps. Grade-separated interchanges cost more but provide better traffic flow than at-grade intersections.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Integral Bents</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A structural engineering support system used on bridges in soft-soil conditions (like western Weber County). Integral bents distribute bridge forces across the structure rather than concentrating them at discrete footings, reducing settlement risks in unstable soil.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Inverse Condemnation</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A legal claim that may be available when a property owner believes government action has taken or damaged property without adequate compensation. Property owners should consult an attorney for legal advice about available remedies.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Right-of-Way</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      The land area required for the highway, including pavement, median, shoulders, and utility areas. The standard right-of-way footprint for SR-177 Weber County extension is 250 feet wide. Properties within the right-of-way are subject to acquisition once the project is funded.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Record of Decision</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      The formal federal and state approval document issued at the conclusion of the State Environmental Study process. The Record of Decision records the selected Build Alternative, responses to public comments, and findings of compliance with environmental laws.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Reasonableness Criterion (Noise)</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      For sound wall mitigation, reasonableness requires that the wall be cost-effective and receive majority support from affected residents through a balloting process. A wall failing the reasonableness criterion will not be constructed, even if feasible.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>State Environmental Study</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      A formal environmental review process required for state highway projects in Utah. The State Environmental Study examines impacts on land use, property, environment, community, and infrastructure. It includes scoping, alternatives evaluation, public hearings, and environmental documentation.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>UDOT</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      Utah Department of Transportation. The state agency responsible for planning, designing, and constructing state highways like SR-177, and for managing property acquisitions, utility relocations, and community engagement.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="glossary-item">
    <div class="glossary-term"><strong>Wasatch Front Regional Council</strong></div>
    <div class="glossary-def">
      The regional planning agency for the Wasatch Front region, responsible for developing and maintaining the Regional Transportation Plan and allocating federal transportation funding.
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<hr class="divider">

<h2>Term Quick Reference</h2>

<ul style="columns: 2; margin: 20px 0;">
  <li><strong>dBA</strong> — Decibels (sound measurement)</li>
  <li><strong>Environmental Impact Statement</strong> — 2017 environmental review used as a Davis County precedent</li>
  <li><strong>Federal Highway Administration</strong> — Federal agency responsible for national highway standards</li>
  <li><strong>I-15</strong> — Interstate 15</li>
  <li><strong>Metropolitan planning organization</strong> — Regional transportation planning body</li>
  <li><strong>Noise Abatement Criteria</strong> — Federal highway noise standards</li>
  <li><strong>Record of Decision</strong> — Final approval document for the environmental review process</li>
  <li><strong>Right-of-way</strong> — Land required for the highway facility</li>
  <li><strong>Regional Transportation Plan</strong> — Long-range regional transportation plan</li>
  <li><strong>State Environmental Study</strong> — Utah environmental review process</li>
  <li><strong>SR-177</strong> — State Route 177 (West Davis Highway)</li>
  <li><strong>UDOT</strong> — Utah Department of Transportation</li>
  <li><strong>Wasatch Front Regional Council</strong> — Regional planning agency for the Wasatch Front</li>
</ul>

